1:
`week2/generated/day2-practice-refresh.spec.ts`:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";

test("the Refresh button says what it should", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

  // Relative XPath: starts //, anchors on the attribute, not on the tree shape.
  const refresh = page.locator("xpath=//button[@id='Button1']");

  await expect(refresh).toContainText("Refresh");
});
```

```bash
npx playwright test week2/generated/day2-practice-refresh.spec.ts
```
→ `1 passed`.

**Relative, not absolute.** `//button[@id='Button1']` says *"a button anywhere with this id"*. The absolute form, `/html/body/div[2]/div/form/div[13]/div/button`, describes a route through the tree, and one wrapper `<div>` added by a redesign sends it somewhere else. Both find the control today; only one survives a layout change.

**Why `toContainText` rather than `toHaveText`.** `toContainText` is a substring check, so it does not care about surrounding whitespace. This page has a real trailing-space problem, which [Day 3 - Fundamentals](/learn/w2/d3/p2) proves on the `<h1>`, and `toHaveText` would make you match it exactly.

**And the honest note the hint is pointing at:** XPath here is an *exercise*, not a recommendation. `getByRole('button', { name: 'Refresh' })` is the locator you would actually ship. XPath is the fallback for when nothing better exists — which is exactly the situation on [Day 4 - Fundamentals](/learn/w2/d4/p2).

2:
```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";
import { fillRegistrationForm } from "./registration-helpers";

test("the form keeps what it was given, and Submit stays on the page", async ({ page }) => {
  const email = "learner.three@example.com";
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, email);

  // Three different fields, three different values.
  await expect(page.getByPlaceholder("First Name")).toHaveValue("Ada");
  await expect(page.locator("input[type='email']")).toHaveValue(email);
  await expect(page.locator("textarea")).toHaveValue("123 Analytical Engine Way");

  await page.locator("#submitbtn").click();

  // This form posts to itself - there is no confirmation page to assert on.
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/Register\.html/);
});
```

**`toHaveValue`, not `toHaveText`.** An `<input>` has no text content — its content is the `value` *property*. `toHaveText` on a filled input matches the empty string and the assertion fails in a way that reads like the fill did not happen.

**Why the `toHaveURL` assertion is the interesting one.** The instinct after clicking Submit is to assert on a confirmation message. This app does not produce one: it posts back to `Register.html` and the fields reset. So the honest thing to assert is what actually happens — you are still on the same page. Asserting on a success banner that does not exist gives you a test that fails for the right reason and the wrong cause, and you lose an afternoon to it.

Writing tests against the app you have, rather than the app you assumed, is most of this day.

